WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.424
Bayesian networks use probability to express uncertainty.

00:00:02.424 --> 00:00:04.416
A probability is not a guarantee.

00:00:04.416 --> 00:00:08.733
It is a way to describe how plausible a state or event is,

00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:11.057
given the model and the information available.

00:00:11.057 --> 00:00:14.709
A prior probability is the belief before new evidence is entered.

00:00:14.709 --> 00:00:15.374
For example,

00:00:15.374 --> 00:00:19.358
a system may have a prior probability of equipment failure based on

00:00:19.358 --> 00:00:22.014
historical maintenance data before any alarm is observed.

00:00:22.014 --> 00:00:25.999
A conditional probability describes the probability of one event given something else.

00:00:25.999 --> 00:00:27.327
The phrase "given" matters.

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:30.647
The probability of an alarm may be low in general,

00:00:30.647 --> 00:00:33.303
but higher given that a sensor fault exists,

00:00:33.303 --> 00:00:35.628
or higher given that equipment has failed.

00:00:35.628 --> 00:00:39.612
Conditional probability lets the model say that context changes what is plausible.

00:00:39.612 --> 00:00:42.268
Evidence is an observation entered into the model.

00:00:42.268 --> 00:00:45.921
An observed alarm, a normal power reading, a failed login pattern,

00:00:45.921 --> 00:00:50.901
or a weather report can all be evidence if the model includes variables for them.

00:00:50.901 --> 00:00:54.553
A posterior probability is the updated belief after considering that evidence.

00:00:54.553 --> 00:00:58.870
The posterior does not become certain unless the model and evidence justify certainty,

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:00.862
which is rare in practical settings.

00:01:00.862 --> 00:01:05.843
A marginal probability summarizes a variable without conditioning on a specific state of another variable.

00:01:05.843 --> 00:01:09.827
A likelihood describes how compatible observed evidence is with a possible explanation.

00:01:09.827 --> 00:01:13.811
Bayes' rule is the conceptual bridge that lets us reverse conditional reasoning.

00:01:13.811 --> 00:01:18.128
A model may know how likely an alert is when a fault exists,

00:01:18.128 --> 00:01:22.112
then use the observed alert to estimate how likely the fault is.

00:01:22.112 --> 00:01:23.440
Base rates are critical.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.101
If a condition is rare,

00:01:25.101 --> 00:01:29.085
a moderately informative signal may raise its probability but still leave it

00:01:29.085 --> 00:01:31.077
less likely than a common alternative.

00:01:31.077 --> 00:01:35.394
Good Bayesian reasoning keeps both the signal and the starting rate in view.

00:01:35.394 --> 00:01:37.054
Normalization is another useful idea.

00:01:37.054 --> 00:01:39.710
After the model combines prior information and evidence,

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:43.694
the candidate probabilities must be scaled so they form a valid distribution.

00:01:43.694 --> 00:01:47.347
In simple terms, the updated possibilities need to add up correctly.

00:01:47.347 --> 00:01:51.663
You do not need to do the arithmetic by hand in this course.

00:01:51.663 --> 00:01:54.652
The practical lesson is that Bayesian updating compares explanations,

00:01:54.652 --> 00:01:59.300
weights them by how well they fit the evidence, and then normalizes the result.
